# AMI LwM2M Node — Application Kconfig

menu "AMI Configuration"

config AMI_SINGLE_PHASE
	bool "Single-phase meter mode"
	default y
	help
	  Enable for single-phase meters (e.g. Microstar residential).
	  Pre-skips Phase S and Phase T OBIS codes at startup to
	  avoid wasting ~5 seconds polling unsupported registers.
	  When disabled (3-phase mode), all 28 OBIS codes are polled.

config AMI_DEMO_MODE
	bool "Demo mode (simulated meter data)"
	default n
	help
	  Run without a physical DLMS/RS485 meter.
	  The node generates realistic synthetic readings and publishes
	  them through LwM2M Object 10242 so end-to-end Thread +
	  ThingsBoard validation can be performed in lab demos.

config AMI_LWM2M_RECOVER_BACKOFF_MIN_S
	int "Initial backoff between LwM2M recovery attempts (seconds)"
	default 60
	help
	  Base of the exponential backoff applied by lwm2m_recover_work_fn
	  when the LwM2M RD client cannot re-register. Doubles each failed
	  attempt up to RECOVER_BACKOFF_MAX_S.

	  60s is conservative enough to give the server time to recover from
	  a GC pause, restart, or temporary network blip without hammering it.

config AMI_LWM2M_RECOVER_BACKOFF_MAX_S
	int "Maximum backoff between LwM2M recovery attempts (seconds)"
	default 300
	help
	  Cap on the exponential backoff. After 60s → 120s → 240s → 300s
	  (capped). Beyond MAX_ATTEMPTS, the node escalates to sys_reboot.

config AMI_LWM2M_RECOVER_MAX_ATTEMPTS
	int "Max LwM2M recovery attempts before sys_reboot (warm)"
	default 10
	help
	  After this many consecutive failed attempts to re-register the
	  LwM2M client, the node performs a SYS_REBOOT_WARM. The Thread
	  session is preserved (warm reboot keeps NVS) so re-attach is fast,
	  but the LwM2M engine state is fully reset. Typical safety net for
	  pathological zombie states (Thread OK but LwM2M wedged).

	  Counter resets ONLY on LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_COMPLETE
	  — UPDATE_COMPLETE does NOT reset it. This is the most conservative
	  policy: if the client repeatedly registers and falls back into
	  failure, escalation still happens.

config AMI_BOOT_REGISTER_DEADLINE_S
	int "Boot watchdog deadline: max seconds between boot and first REGISTER COMPLETE"
	default 90
	help
	  PRIO 8 boot watchdog. From the moment the firmware enters main loop
	  to the first LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_COMPLETE event, this
	  is the maximum allowed time. If exceeded, the node performs a
	  SYS_REBOOT_WARM (preserving Thread NVS).

	  This is defense-in-depth for Bug E: nodes that hang in the boot
	  path before reaching lwm2m_rd_client_start (Thread attach stuck,
	  DNS-SD lookup repeatedly failing, init crash, etc) will auto-recover
	  without operator intervention.

	  Default 90s budget: 30s Thread attach + 5s IPv6 propagation +
	  10x7s DNS-SD retries + 30s REGISTER + safety margin. If actual
	  measured boot time exceeds this, raise the value before lowering
	  the watchdog gracefully.

	  Setting to 0 disables the boot watchdog (not recommended in fleet).

config AMI_LWM2M_SEND
	bool "Push billing/PQ resources via LwM2M Send (1.1) instead of relying on server Observe"
	default n
	depends on LWM2M_VERSION_1_1
	help
	  When y, after each DLMS poll the client proactively SENDs the
	  minimal AMI resource set (voltage, active power, power factor,
	  active energy, frequency) to the server using the LwM2M 1.1 Send
	  operation. This GUARANTEES per-poll cadence regardless of whether
	  the server successfully established an Observe on each resource.

	  Motivation (2026-06-11): TB Edge's per-resource Observe/Write-
	  Attributes application is unreliable — voltage and activePower
	  observes failed to establish on a 30-node fleet, so their notifies
	  went nowhere and TB showed them frozen at the registration read
	  while frequency/powerFactor (whose observes did establish) updated
	  every minute. Send removes the dependency on the server-side
	  Observe entirely: the firmware owns the cadence.

config AMI_TX_POWER_DBM
	int "OpenThread TX power (dBm)"
	default 4
	range -16 20
	help
	  Power output for the IEEE 802.15.4 radio. Lower values reduce the
	  ESP32-C6 self-heating significantly: each +3 dBm roughly doubles
	  RF output and adds ~20-30 mA of current draw during TX bursts,
	  which translates directly to die temperature.

	  History:
	    v0.6.22: 8 dBm (original).
	    v0.6.23: 0 dBm (thermal pack). Cut self-heating ~85% but field
	             soak with 30 nodes showed 1141 TxDirectMaxRetryExpiry
	             events — marginal link quality was costing UPDATEs and
	             cascading into REG_TIMEOUT → recover_work → reboot loops.
	    v0.6.25: 4 dBm. Middle ground: ~50% of the v0.6.23 thermal benefit
	             retained, but the link-quality margin recovered. Expect
	             retry-expiry rate to drop by >5x at this density.

	  Raise to 8 for spread deployments (>5 m), 20 for outdoor/long-range.

config AMI_MINIMAL_AMI
	bool "Minimalist AMI: keep only Power Meter + FW update"
	default n
	help
	  Skip all non-essential LwM2M object setups so the periodic observe
	  pushes shrink to just V / I / P from the Power Meter object. Used to
	  keep the LwM2M TX burst short enough that USB host overcurrent
	  protection doesn't trip the chronic VBUS-cut events on SuperMini boards.

	  Objects DISABLED when y:
	    - 3303 IPSO Temperature
	    - 33000 thread/lwm2m diagnostic (36 RIDs)
	    - 33001 Thread Role Control
	    - 3311 Light Control
	    - 10483-10486 Thread net/neighbor/commission/cli

	  Objects KEPT:
	    - 10242 Power Meter (V / I / P)
	    - 5     Firmware Update (OTA)
	    - 3     Device (standard)

config AMI_BRN_TEST_NO_LWM2M
	bool "Diag: skip LwM2M client start (brownout isolation test)"
	default n
	help
	  When y, main.c skips lwm2m_rd_client_start() and all observe/register
	  paths. Thread still attaches, watchdogs still run, NVS still writes
	  occasional counters. Used to isolate whether LwM2M CoAP block
	  transfers / observe pushes / register exchanges are what triggers
	  the chronic USB-VBUS-cut events on SuperMini boards.

config AMI_POST_BRN_REDUCE_TX
	bool "Reduce TX power after a brownout reset"
	default n
	help
	  Mirrors Espressif's CONFIG_ESP_PHY_REDUCE_TX_POWER (which Zephyr's
	  SoC layer does not source). At boot, if the post-mortem rr is BRN
	  (last reset was brownout), main.c drops the OpenThread TX power to
	  AMI_POST_BRN_TX_POWER_DBM for this boot. The board re-boots into
	  weak-TX mode, makes the next radio bursts much less likely to dip
	  the rail again, and recovers without sliding into another BOD reset.
	  On a clean boot (POR/SW/WDOG) the standard AMI_TX_POWER_DBM is used.

config AMI_POST_BRN_TX_POWER_DBM
	int "TX power after BRN reset (dBm)"
	default -8
	range -16 4
	depends on AMI_POST_BRN_REDUCE_TX
	help
	  Floor TX power applied for one boot after the BOD trips. -8 dBm cuts
	  the radio peak current by roughly 60 mA vs +4 dBm. Range is short
	  (a few meters indoor) but enough to remain attached to a nearby
	  parent and let the chip stabilize before the next clean reboot.

config AMI_BOOT_JITTER_MAX_S
	int "Random jitter (s) before initial REGISTER on cold boot"
	default 30
	range 0 120
	help
	  v0.6.25 anti-thundering-herd. After 30+ nodes power-on simultaneously
	  (PSU cycle, breaker flip, etc.) the previous boot path tried to
	  REGISTER at the same wall-clock second on every node. Result: mesh
	  contention at wpan0, race conditions in the TB Edge "Profile null"
	  bug, and a higher first-REGISTER failure rate that snowballed into
	  recover loops.

	  Each node now sleeps a random number of seconds in [0, AMI_BOOT_JITTER_MAX_S]
	  between lwm2m_setup() and lwm2m_rd_client_start(). The random seed
	  comes from sys_rand32_get() which is HW-RNG-backed on ESP32-C6 so
	  every node sees a different value. Spreads the first-REGISTER
	  attempt over a wider window, reduces simultaneous-boot REG_TIMEOUTs.

	  Set 0 to disable (single-node lab work / unit testing).

config AMI_HW_WATCHDOG_BOOT_GRACE_HARD_S
	int "Hard cap on hw_watchdog boot grace (seconds)"
	default 600
	range 60 3600
	help
	  v0.6.25 closes a critical recovery gap. hw_watchdog.c kernel feeder
	  thread keeps feeding the TG0_WDT channel WHILE the node has never
	  received a server-ACKed event (last_liveness_uptime == 0). This is
	  intentional boot grace so the Thread attach + first REGISTER cycle
	  is not cut short.

	  But: if a node post-reboot fails to register for any reason
	  (DNS-SD timeout, mesh re-attach mid-flight, the v0.6.x
	  thread_diag_res buffer overflow, etc.), it could stay in boot
	  grace FOREVER — TG0_WDT would never bite. We observed this in the
	  v0.6.24 soak: 14 nodes stayed dead-on-arrival with watchdog_count=0
	  for hours.

	  This config caps boot grace: past this many seconds since boot
	  WITHOUT a single liveness event, the kernel feeder stops feeding
	  unconditionally → TG0_WDT bites at the hardware level → SOC reset.
	  Fully recoverable from kernel-stalled / panic-handler-broken
	  scenarios that defeat every software watchdog.

	  600 s = 10 min default. Lower for faster recovery; raise if your
	  Thread attach is genuinely slow.

config AMI_RGB_AUTO_OFF_AFTER_REGISTER_S
	int "Seconds to keep RGB LED green after first REGISTER, then auto-off"
	default 0
	help
	  Defense-in-depth against self-heating from the WS2812 RGB LED.
	  After the first LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_COMPLETE the
	  LED stays green for this many seconds, then turns OFF to save
	  power and reduce die heating. Errors (RED) and boot states
	  (BLUE/CYAN) still drive the LED — only steady-state GREEN is
	  auto-turned-off.

	  Set 0 to keep the LED ON forever (legacy behavior).

config AMI_REBOOT_USB_DRAIN_MS
	int "Milliseconds to sleep before sys_reboot to drain USB Serial/JTAG"
	default 5000
	help
	  ESP32-C6 native USB Serial/JTAG often hangs (host-side enumerated
	  but cannot open) when sys_reboot() races with an active console
	  session. Sleeping before the reboot gives the host time to detect
	  link drop and clean up its USB stack so the post-reset enumeration
	  succeeds without manual unplug.

	  5000 ms is conservative; 1500-2000 ms also works in practice but
	  loses log buffer flushing margin. Set to 0 to disable the delay.

config AMI_LWM2M_NOTIFY_MIN_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Per-resource minimum interval between LwM2M Notifies (milliseconds)"
	default 30000
	help
	  Firmware-side defense-in-depth throttle: each LwM2M resource of the
	  3-Phase Power Meter Object (10242) emits at most one Notify every
	  this-many milliseconds, regardless of how fast the DLMS poll updates
	  values or whether the server has configured pmin via Write-Attributes.

	  Standard pattern in industrial deployments (Apple HomeKit, Nordic
	  nRF Cloud) — independent of any server-side rate control.

	  Sane values:
	    30000  default. ~1 notify per resource per 30s. With 14 resources
	           and 30 nodes ≈ 14/min/node × 30 = 420/min total to TB Edge.
	    60000  more conservative for very large fleets (>60 nodes).
	    < 5000 aggressive — can saturate the Thread mesh with >20 nodes.
	    0      disable throttle (NOT recommended for production).

	  When the server eventually sends Write-Attributes with pmin, the
	  effective rate is MAX(server_pmin, this_local_minimum). Our path
	  always checks this minimum first; Zephyr's LwM2M engine independently
	  honors any pmin attribute set on the resource. So this Kconfig acts
	  as a hard floor.

	  Per-mesh override: set in overlays/<mesh>.conf if needed.

config AMI_LWM2M_SERVER_IPV6_PRIMARY
	string "DEPRECATED — Primary LwM2M server IPv6 (no longer read by code)"
	default ""
	help
	  DEPRECATED in v0.6.0. Server discovery is DNS-SD only via
	  _lwm2m._udp.default.service.arpa. The firmware ignores this value
	  even if set. The Kconfig symbol is preserved so legacy overlays
	  do not fail to build, but its value has no effect.

	  Remove from your overlay file. v0.7.0 will delete the symbol.

config AMI_LWM2M_SERVER_IPV6_SECONDARY
	string "DEPRECATED — Secondary LwM2M server IPv6 (no longer read by code)"
	default ""
	help
	  DEPRECATED in v0.6.0. See AMI_LWM2M_SERVER_IPV6_PRIMARY.

config AMI_CONN_MONITOR_WDOG_S
	int "conn_monitor cycle-silence watchdog threshold (v0.6.73)"
	default 300
	range 60 1800
	help
	  After the first conn_monitor cycle has fired, the cycle is expected
	  to run at least every CONN_UPDATE_INTERVAL_S (~60 s) with jitter. If
	  the gap between cycles exceeds this threshold, the main loop is
	  wedged and the board is COLD-rebooted.

	  Catches the "alive but mute" zombie state where Thread/LwM2M
	  REG_UPDATE keeps refreshing the silence_watchdog (it's fed by
	  REGISTRATION_COMPLETE / REG_UPDATE_COMPLETE events), but the
	  main-loop conn_monitor thread has wedged on a deadlock, stack
	  overflow, or similar — so telemetry stops flowing while TB still
	  marks the board active.

	  Default 300 s (5 cycles). Lower for aggressive recovery; raise to
	  ~600 s in environments where conn_monitor legitimately stalls on
	  expensive operations (large meter polls, blocking peripherals).

config AMI_CONN_MONITOR_BOOT_GRACE_S
	int "conn_monitor watchdog boot grace (v0.6.73)"
	default 240
	range 60 900
	help
	  Until the first conn_monitor cycle has fired, the watchdog silently
	  heartbeats — first cycle requires Thread attach + DNS-SD + REGISTER
	  and can legitimately take >60 s on slow attaches. After this many
	  seconds with zero cycles, the watchdog declares the boot wedged and
	  forces a COLD reboot.

	  Default 240 s — generous for normal field attaches but still bounded.

config AMI_BOOT_STAGGER_MAX_MS
	int "Random boot-stagger window in milliseconds (v0.6.72)"
	default 30000
	range 0 120000
	help
	  Maximum random delay (uniform 0..MAX) applied at the very start of
	  main(), BEFORE any peripheral init. Prevents coordinated boot inrush
	  when N boards share a power rail or USB hub: without stagger, all N
	  boards reach Thread radio init within milliseconds of each other,
	  causing simultaneous current peaks that brownout the shared rail and
	  trigger cascading reboot loops.

	  Set 0 to disable (single-board lab work). 30000 (30 s) is the
	  field-tested default — even with 30 boards on one rail, the chance
	  of two hitting radio init in the same 100 ms window is <0.3%.

	  Costs 0..MAX ms of additional boot latency per board. Operator-
	  invisible because boards are not registered to TB during boot;
	  they appear "boot stagger" delayed in TB only as a later first
	  registration, which is what the existing watchdog logic expects.

	  Requires CONFIG_ENTROPY_GENERATOR=y for actual randomness; otherwise
	  sys_rand32_get() returns deterministic values and stagger collapses.

config AMI_BOOT_BURST_MAX
	int "Boot-burst limiter threshold — consecutive unstable boots (v0.6.71)"
	default 10
	range 3 50
	help
	  Anti-brick rate limiter. The boot_burst counter is incremented on
	  every boot, and reset to 0 only when the board reaches a sustained-
	  healthy state (see CONFIG_AMI_OTA_CONFIRM_DELAY_S — same gate).
	  If the counter reaches this threshold before reset, the next boot
	  performs CONFIG_AMI_BOOT_BURST_THROTTLE_S of pre-init sleep before
	  touching anything else.

	  Purpose: bound the NVS-write rate so a fast crash loop cannot wear
	  out flash. ESP32-C6 NVS sectors are rated for ~100k erase cycles.
	  Without this limiter, a board crashing every 5 min could hit that
	  limit in <1 year. The throttle also gives operators a quiet window
	  to physically intervene without the board destroying itself.

	  Default 10 — needs to be higher than the noreg_boots cap (5) so the
	  noreg_boots backoff has room to do its work first. Lower (e.g. 5)
	  for aggressive wear protection on volatile field deployments;
	  higher (e.g. 20) for development boards where crashes are expected.

config AMI_BOOT_BURST_THROTTLE_S
	int "Pre-init throttle sleep when boot-burst limit hit (v0.6.71)"
	default 300
	range 30 1800
	help
	  Seconds the firmware sleeps at the very start of main() when the
	  boot-burst counter is at or above CONFIG_AMI_BOOT_BURST_MAX. Long
	  enough that an external observer notices the board is in
	  protective-throttle mode (no LED activity, no log output) and can
	  physically intervene. Also clamps the NVS-write rate to at most
	  one cycle per (this delay + boot time).

	  Default 300 s (5 min). Operators see "board boots once every 5 min"
	  in TB Edge attribute updates and know to investigate.

config AMI_OTA_CONFIRM_DELAY_S
	int "Sustained-connectivity delay before MCUboot confirm (v0.6.71)"
	default 600
	range 60 3600
	help
	  After the first REGISTRATION_COMPLETE event on a fresh OTA image,
	  the firmware schedules a delayed work that re-checks lwm2m_connected
	  AND keepalive_emit_count >= 1 before writing the MCUboot confirm
	  flag. If either check fails at trigger time (or the board reboots
	  before this delay elapses), MCUboot reverts to the prior image on
	  next boot — automatic rollback for OTAs that pass the first
	  REGISTER but fail sustained operation (DLMS deadlock, late race,
	  late OOM, etc.).

	  Default 600 s (10 min) — long enough for at least 2 keepalive
	  cycles (~300 s each) so we have strong evidence of sustained
	  health. Lower bound 60 s for testing, upper bound 3600 s for
	  conservative deployments.

config AMI_MESH_ALONE_MAX_S
	int "Max cumulative Thread-detached seconds before COLD reboot (v0.6.71)"
	default 3600
	range 300 14400
	help
	  Mesh-alone watchdog threshold. If the board spends this many cumulative
	  seconds in OT_DEVICE_ROLE_DETACHED across the lifetime of the boot,
	  the radio/mesh stack is considered wedged and a COLD reboot is
	  triggered. This complements the LwM2M silence watchdog (which fires
	  on emit silence) and noreg_boots throttle (which prevents reboot
	  storms on server outages). Mesh-alone catches the case where the
	  Thread attach itself is broken — no other layer covers that.

	  Default 3600 s (1 hour) — generous for steady-state operation where
	  a healthy board spends ~0 s detached after first attach. Lower (e.g.
	  600 s) for aggressive recovery on volatile RF environments; higher
	  (e.g. 7200 s) when an OTBR outage is expected to be long-lived and
	  reboots are wasteful.

config AMI_HW_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Hardware watchdog channel timeout (seconds)"
	default 180
	range 30 600
	help
	  PRIO 9 hardware watchdog (TG0_WDT via task_wdt). Per-channel timeout
	  in seconds. If any channel goes unfed for this long, the SoC is
	  hard-reset (SYS_RESET). Two channels are armed:

	    kernel-alive  fed from a dedicated K_PRIO_PREEMPT(4) thread.
	                  Times out only if the kernel scheduler is stalled
	                  or all threads above prio 4 are starving lower ones.

	    workq-alive   fed from a self-rescheduling delayed_work on
	                  system_workq. Times out if the system workq is dead
	                  (deadlocked work item, lost worker thread, etc).

	  180 s is comfortably above any legitimate blocking the application
	  performs (DNS-SD lookups ≤ 7 s × N retries, LwM2M restart cycles ≤
	  60 s, ami_reboot_drain ≤ 5 s). Lower only if you've measured the
	  worst-case feed latency on hardware and know it stays well below
	  the new value.

	  Range 30..600 s. The HW timer runs continuously from boot, including
	  during Thread attach (typical 10–30 s), so any value below ~60 s
	  risks false positives at boot.

config AMI_REAL_LIVENESS_TIMEOUT_S
	int "Real-liveness watchdog: max seconds without a server-ACKed LwM2M event"
	default 600
	range 180 3600
	help
	  PRIO 9 backstop, v0.6.17. The hardware watchdog's kernel-alive
	  channel is gated on this: if no LWM2M_RD_CLIENT_EVENT_REGISTRATION_
	  COMPLETE or REG_UPDATE_COMPLETE arrives for this many seconds AFTER
	  the first registration, the node is "registered but cut off" and the
	  kernel feeder triggers a cold SoC reset.

	  This catches the failure mode the original watchdog could not: a
	  wedged USB/radio peripheral while the CPU stays healthy. The kernel
	  scheduler keeps running, both watchdog channels keep being fed, yet
	  no data reaches the server. Field proof: a node hung ~1.5 h with
	  total_resets unchanged — zero automatic recovery.

	  A server-ACKed registration event is the only un-fakeable liveness
	  signal: it requires the full radio + mesh + routing + server + CoAP
	  path to be alive. lwm2m_set_*/notify success does NOT — it only
	  queues data locally.

	  Default 600 s (10 min): comfortably longer than the soft LwM2M
	  watchdog's full detect+recover escalation (~240 s detect, then
	  backoff retries), so it only fires when that softer layer has
	  already failed. With lifetime 120 s, a healthy node gets a
	  REG_UPDATE_COMPLETE roughly every 90 s, so 600 s tolerates several
	  missed updates + CoAP retransmit chains before declaring the node
	  dead. Lower it for faster recovery if false positives are not seen
	  in the field; raise it if a slow server (GC pauses) trips it.

config AMI_LWM2M_DNS_SD_RETRY_MAX
	int "Maximum DNS-SD lookup retries before sys_reboot at boot"
	default 10
	help
	  Number of consecutive DNS-SD failures at boot before the node
	  performs sys_reboot(SYS_REBOOT_WARM). Each retry uses exponential
	  backoff (5s, 10s, 20s, ..., capped at 60s). Default 10 → max ~9 min
	  before reboot. Tunable for harsh mesh-attach environments.

config AMI_COAP_KEEPALIVE_PERIOD_S
	int "CoAP keepalive period (seconds)"
	default 300
	range 60 3600
	help
	  Period at which src/coap_keepalive.c fires a Notify on
	  /33000/0/uptime_s. The Zephyr LwM2M engine emits a confirmable CoAP
	  message every time; on a dead socket the 3-retry budget exhausts in
	  ~270 s and triggers lwm2m_rd_client_timeout() -> automatic re-
	  registration. Without this we'd have to wait 2 * LIFETIME (= 48 h
	  with the v0.6.66 CONFIG_LWM2M_ENGINE_DEFAULT_LIFETIME=86400) for
	  the silence watchdog. 300 s is the sweet spot: short enough that
	  TB Edge restart recovery converges in <5 min, long enough that
	  pmin/pmax write-attributes (typically pmin=30, pmax=300 on Object
	  33000) never suppress us.

config AMI_LWM2M_DNS_SD_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Per-attempt DNS-SD lookup timeout (milliseconds)"
	default 7000
	help
	  Timeout for a single otDnsClientResolveService attempt. Below 5000
	  may starve under realistic mesh latency; above 15000 causes
	  sluggish boot if SRP server is unresponsive.

choice AMI_MESH
	prompt "Target Thread mesh"
	default AMI_MESH_R1000
	help
	  Selects which OTBR/Thread network this firmware will join.
	  The dataset TLV is hardcoded in src/main.c (apply_otbr_dataset)
	  to bypass NVS persistence — switching here selects which TLV is
	  compiled in.

	  Use overlays to combine with the device-role variant:
	    west build ... -- -DEXTRA_CONF_FILE="overlays/med.conf;overlays/r1000.conf"
	  or via tools/build_firmware.py:
	    python tools/build_firmware.py --variant med --mesh r1000

config AMI_MESH_R1000
	bool "UNAL-R1000 on Seeed R1000 (192.168.8.176, channel 21) — production default"
	help
	  Production deployment — clean channel, lessons learned applied.
	    Channel:        21
	    PAN ID:         0x41ae
	    Network Name:   UNAL-R1000
	    Mesh-local:     fdf1:a391:6243:2a67::/64
	    LwM2M server:   fdf1:a391:6243:2a67:2478:c089:bf5a:2554 (mleid R1000)
	    LwM2M lifetime: 120s (set in overlays/r1000.conf)
	  See docs/architecture/edge-r1000.md for full spec.

config AMI_MESH_PI4
	bool "UNAL-Thread on Pi4 EKH01 (192.168.1.111, channel 25) — legacy"
	help
	  Legacy deployment — kept for migration of existing nodes.
	    Channel:        25
	    PAN ID:         0x23ed
	    Network Name:   UNAL-Thread
	    Mesh-local:     fdf5:bffd:0bd6:ef74::/64
	    LwM2M server:   fdf5:bffd:bd6:ef74:eb7b:f1d1:874e:2b91 (mleid Pi4)
	    LwM2M lifetime: 300s (prj.conf default, no per-mesh override)
	  Note: this network has documented contention with rogue PAN 0xe702
	  on channel 25 — see docs/architecture/edge-thingsboard.md.

endchoice

config AMI_LED_QUIET_MODE
	bool "Production-quiet LED mode"
	default y
	help
	  Suppress all firmware-driven LED state (boot UI, TX-blink, detach-RED).
	  Only Object 3311 server-driven writes via ami_led_set_raw() can light
	  the LED, so the fleet looks visually identical (all OFF) by default.
	  Recommended for production deployments — reduces visual noise, removes
	  WS2812 current cost, and avoids the "some green / some off" effect
	  caused by nodes being in different lifecycle phases.
	  Disable (=n) only for QA/development where you want boot-state visibility.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
